106
00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:41.390
Arun S M: Thanks, Rafa, for putting together this proposal. I do have a question, quick question.

107
00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.890
Arun S M: In terms of ensuring that,

108
00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.199
Arun S M: people do adhere to this, right? For instance.

109
00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:55.179
Arun S M: Let's talk about code attribution. Generally, we require… I mean, not generally, but we do require people.

110
00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:59.350
Arun S M: whoever is contributing to have a… developer certificate.

111
00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:01.740
Arun S M: Origin through a sign-off message.

112
00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:07.370
Arun S M: And, does the tooling today do… does support Noted.

113
00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:12.399
Arun S M: Such capabilities, where we can covering this?

114
00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:18.589
Arun S M: Through tooling, as opposed to… like, or is it a responsibility of…

115
00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:27.490
Arun S M: A maintainer to figure out if the code contribution is indeed from from a GenAI source, right?

116
00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:31.940
Arun S M: And second question is also on the,

117
00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:36.779
Arun S M: This is a source or a reference that Gen AI might have used.

118
00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:46.150
Arun S M: In terms of, like, Keeping, keeping the licensing terms right, or, like, ensuring that it is not

119
00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:51.480
Arun S M: Infringing on an existing license, or it might have not been trained on something.

120
00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:57.320
Arun S M: Do you think we need some policies or some legal sentence that will

121
00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:04.220
Arun S M: Protect developers, or that will protect the project, or in general, that will protect LFTT.

122
00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.170
Arun S M: So, any thoughts on these two questions?

123
00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:14.900
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Thank you for your questions. Those are two very good questions. Regarding the first one.

124
00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:29.259
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Well, there's a lot of scientific research on trying to assure provenance of a piece of code, or a flow of execution. An area that comes to mind is the trusted execution environments.

125
00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:36.790
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Where you have an isolated memory area, and you have isolated private keys, and you ensure some computation is done.

126
00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:42.459
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): I think that to enforce a DCO that comes from the LLM side.

127
00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:56.539
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): we would probably need changes to how these tools are currently working. As far as I know, there's no such constraint or enforcement in their backend, right? So, if we wanted to

128
00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:10.800
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): proof that a certain piece of code that was incorporated in an open source project was generated by an LLM, probably the LLM would need to return the response with a signature, so it could be verified later, or so on.

129
00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:17.319
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): So technically, it can be done. In practice, I don't think most providers have that.

130
00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:19.240
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): That option on the table.

131
00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:35.869
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): And even if they did, you know, a user can copy the response and then assign them themselves. We would have to think of a protocol to do that. So a more simple and straightforward way to solve the problem is that we defer that responsibility to the contributor, but we have no way to prove if

132
00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.419
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): they used an LLM or not, right?

133
00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:48.139
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): So that comes into the personal responsibility area, right? We trust the contributor and the maintainer to responsibly disclose

134
00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:53.539
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): their usage of the tools. I think that's a good starting point, I don't know what the Foundation thinks.

135
00:15:53.860 --> 00:16:00.529
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): But generally speaking, we… You know, we pass the ball to the… to the volunteer, to the contributor.

136
00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:08.240
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Second question was… Would you please remind me of the second question?

137
00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:12.600
Arun S M: Sure, so in terms of license protections.

138
00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:17.220
Arun S M: Like, how do we ensure that this is a source that

139
00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:25.020
Arun S M: These genii sources are referring to, they are not infringed upon already, or… Oh, who… who's…

140
00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.489
Arun S M: Who's responsible on if such a thing is produced?

141
00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:33.250
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Alright, so here's a very uncomfortable question for me, because…

142
00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:41.360
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): it really touches legal. I think the Foundation should consider this legally, but technically, what I can say is that

143
00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:50.880
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): The code that's generated, Could be coming from any project, any codebase, and there could be similarities with

144
00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.659
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Projects that have other licenses.

145
00:16:53.810 --> 00:17:01.370
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): In practice, if the code is for a very specific function and usage, the probability of intersection is rather low.

146
00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:12.139
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): If it's something very abstract, or, sorry, rather very well established, for example, give me a function that

147
00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:18.299
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): implements bubble sort in C. That's surely implemented in an open source library somewhere.

148
00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:26.259
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): It could be copied with very few modifications, and it… someone could be making the case that that code

149
00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:34.190
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): It's part of a well-known repository for a long time, and it's, you know, actually copyright infringement.

150
00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:45.100
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Practically, in practice, we… We use these tools for very specific purposes, tailored to the specific project.

151
00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:52.829
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): And then, contributors are obviously… Incentivized to do their own review, and with that, changing the code.

152
00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.490
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Such that it minimizes the likelihood of

153
00:17:56.990 --> 00:18:03.059
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): you know, just being copying code directly. But this is a wider problem, right? Not only for our…

154
00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:06.559
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): Industry, also arts, music, and so on.

155
00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:13.810
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): So I'm afraid I don't have a good answer for you, except that let's try to be… not…

156
00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:17.950
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): blindly copy-pasting codes that LLMs provide.

157
00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:23.930
Arun S M: Thanks, Rafael.

158
00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:27.010
Rafael  Belchior (INESC-ID): You're welcome.

